# Zensar Banner Studio

An AI banner generator where the user controls the message and the picture, and
the brand stays fixed. Gemini produces the artwork; the backend composes the
type and drops the Zensar marks into two locked corners on every single banner.

```
brief ──▶ planner (Gemini text) ──▶ BannerSpec ──▶ image model (Gemini image)
                                         │                    │
                                         └────▶ composer (Pillow) ◀── artwork
                                                     │
                                          locked logos + type
                                                     │
                                                 final PNG
```

The image model is never asked to draw text or a logo. That is the whole
design: it is the only way the top-right and bottom-right marks land in the
same place at the same size on every banner.

## Layout

```
backend/
  main.py                    FastAPI routes
  config.py                  models, canvas sizes, brand palette, asset paths
  models.py                  BannerSpec and request/response schemas
  services/
    prompt_engine.py         brief -> BannerSpec, spec -> image prompt
    image_gen.py             Gemini image generation and editing
    composer.py              scrim, type, accent rule, locked logo placement
    templates.py             layout geometry as fractions of the canvas
  assets/brand/              logo files go here (see the README inside)
  assets/fonts/              optional brand fonts
  outputs/                   generated banners + their specs

frontend/
  src/App.tsx                state and orchestration
  src/api.ts                 typed API client
  src/types.ts               shared types
  src/styles.css             design tokens and layout
  src/components/
    ControlDeck.tsx          brief, copy, layout, uploads, advanced controls
    BannerStage.tsx          preview, locked-zone overlay, edit bar, download
    TemplatePicker.tsx       layout chooser with wireframe glyphs
    ImageDrop.tsx            reference / person / background uploads
    ModeTabs.tsx             create · reference · edit
    SpecInspector.tsx        the plan behind the banner
```

## Run it

Backend:

```bash
cd backend
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
cp .env.example .env          # add your GEMINI_API_KEY
uvicorn main:app --reload --port 8000
```

Frontend:

```bash
cd frontend
npm install
cp .env.example .env
npm run dev                   # http://localhost:5173
```

Without an API key the app still runs: the planner falls back to keyword
planning and the image step returns brand-coloured artwork, so you can develop
and test composition, fonts and logo placement offline.

## Installing the logo

Put the marks in `backend/assets/brand/` as `logo-black.png`, `logo-white.png`,
`footer-black.png`, `footer-white.png` — or drop one file into the "Install
logo" prompt in the app. The composer samples the brightness behind each corner
and picks the light or dark version automatically. If a file is missing it
draws a text mark and says so in the banner's warnings.

To move the marks, edit `LOGO_ZONE` and `FOOTER_ZONE` in
`backend/services/templates.py`. The preview overlay in `BannerStage.tsx`
mirrors those two constants — update both together.

## Three modes

- **Create** — a brief becomes a banner.
- **Match a reference** — upload a banner, say what to keep and what to change.
  The reference and the portrait both go to the image model as inputs.
- **Edit** — one instruction re-runs the artwork and recomposes. Text and
  layout changes skip the model entirely via `/api/recompose`, so fixing a
  headline is instant and free.

## API

| Route                | Does                                                     |
|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------|
| `GET  /api/health`   | Key status, brand name, missing brand assets             |
| `GET  /api/templates`| Available layouts                                        |
| `POST /api/plan`     | Interpret a brief without generating an image            |
| `POST /api/generate` | Full pipeline, returns the banner and its spec           |
| `POST /api/edit`     | Re-run artwork with an instruction, recompose            |
| `POST /api/recompose`| Re-render type and layout over existing artwork          |
| `POST /api/brand-asset` | Install a logo into a brand slot                      |
| `GET  /media/*`      | Generated files                                          |

## Notes on models

Image model names live in `config.py` and are read from the environment, since
Google renames these often. Check the current Gemini image-generation
documentation before deploying and set `IMAGE_MODEL_FAST`, `IMAGE_MODEL_STANDARD`
and `IMAGE_MODEL_PRO` accordingly. Nothing in the pipeline hard-codes a model.

## Adding a layout

Add an entry to `TEMPLATES` in `backend/services/templates.py`. Every rect is
`(x, y, w, h)` as a fraction of the canvas, so one definition works at all five
aspect ratios. The frontend picks up the new layout automatically — the
wireframe glyph is drawn from the template's own text and subject positions.

## Before production

- Move `outputs/` to object storage and serve signed URLs.
- Add auth, per-user history and rate limiting on the generate route.
- Cache specs so a re-render never costs a second planner call.
- Install the brand fonts in `assets/fonts/` and point `FONTS` at them;
  the composer falls back to system fonts until you do.

